tests: Remove use of mutable NetApp fakes

Backport of upstream commit cc981d81b6 (master, 2025-09-17,
Change-Id I50c45a0b21db7f36f1ff08f27fb434587a80210f). Cinder 28.0.0
contains it, 27.0.0 does not, and no 27.0.x tag exists that could be
pinned instead.

`CapabilitiesLibraryTestCase.test_update_ssc`
(cinder/tests/unit/volume/drivers/netapp/dataontap/utils/test_capabilities.py)
is a ddt test with the variants `_1_nfs` and `_2_iscsi`. Both hand the
module-level dicts `fake.SSC_AGGREGATE_INFO['volume1']` and `['volume2']`
to the mocked `_get_ssc_aggregate_info` by reference, and `update_ssc`
(cinder/volume/drivers/netapp/dataontap/utils/capabilities.py) pops
`netapp_node_name` off the dict it receives. The first variant to run in
a process strips the key from the shared fake, and the second one fails
with `KeyError: 'netapp_node_name'`.

Whether the two variants share a process is a coin toss. stestr builds
the id list it partitions from a `set` (stestr/selection.py,
construct_list), so with no timing data the round-robin over the
workers follows the per-process string hash order. With the four
workers of the test-service-images job both variants land on the same
worker in about one run out of four; within a worker the order is the
discovery order, so it is always `_2_iscsi` that fails. Seven of the
eight red `test-service-images (cinder, 2025.2)` jobs between
2026-09-09 and 2026-09-13 were this failure, two of them on main. The
2026.1 leg never hit it.

The upstream change moves the single-consumer fakes inline into the
test method, so every run starts from a fresh dict. In 27.0.0 only
fakes.py and test_capabilities.py reference the removed constants. With
the patch, `stestr run --concurrency 1` on the module passes both
variants in one process, and the whole
cinder.tests.unit.volume.drivers.netapp package stays green (1691
passed, 2 skipped). No 2026.1 twin: 28.0.0 already carries the commit.

Upstream status: merged on master as cc981d81b6, backported to
stable/2025.2 as 7faebca9b5 (2026-02-27, Closes-bug: #2125159).

